use std
into cloth.amni

Imports: os, binary, gfx, math, gfx.linalg, gfx.gltf, gfx.motion, gfx.scene3d and spring (the local module spring.amni; its `mquat`, `pos`, `qconj` and `clamp01` are used unqualified). This file is a module with no main. It simulates garment fabric: each garment group has a cage of points that are skin joints, the render mesh is skinned to those joints, and each frame this module moves the points with position-based dynamics (gravity and the model's wind, distance and bend constraints, tethers to the pinned points, a maximum swing from where the body's skin would carry the point, contact with the body's high points near each point and with its nearest limb capsules), then poses each joint with the point's position and the rotation that best matches its neighbours. Rigid and tight fabric is not simulated at all: it stays skinned to the body and costs nothing. Normalize every vector with `linalg::unit3`; rotate vectors with `linalg::qrotate`; points through matrices with `linalg::point`.

Fabric classes are data, built once into a module `var` from this table (struct `Fabric { name byte[>..], sim bool, cell f32, gain f32, swing f32, bend f32, damp f32, grav f32 }`): sim says whether the class is simulated, cell is the cage spacing, a point's largest swing is `min(swing, gain * max(gap - 0.01, 0))` with gap its rest distance from the body, bend is the bend-edge stiffness, damp the velocity kept per substep and grav the share of gravity.

```table fabric_rows Fabric
rigid|false|0|0|0|0|0|0
tight|false|0|0|0|0|0|0
close|true|0.04|2.0|0.04|0.3|0.98|1.0
loose|true|0.025|3.0|0.15|0.15|0.985|1.0
```

Which mesh is which class, and which meshes share a cage (a group), is also data (struct `Wear { mesh byte[>..], group byte[>..], class byte[>..] }`), built once into a module `var` from this table, then extended by the lines of an optional per-model file (each line: mesh, group and class separated by spaces; a line for a mesh already listed replaces its row):

```table wear_rows Wear
skirt|skirt|loose
tank|tank|tight
tank_hem|tank|close
shorts|shorts|tight
shorts_lace|shorts|tight
socks|socks|tight
stripes|socks|tight
gloves|gloves|tight
belt|belt|rigid
buckle|belt|rigid
choker|choker|rigid
plate|choker|rigid
chain|chain|rigid
shoes|shoes|rigid
glasses|glasses|rigid
outfit|outfit|tight
hoodie|hoodie|close
hood|hoodie|close
hood_hem|hoodie|close
pocket|hoodie|close
seam|hoodie|close
collar|hoodie|close
```

Module state: `var npts = 0`, `var item = -1`, `var sk = -1`, `var ready = false`, `var wind = float3 { 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 }` (a force per unit mass the page may set, meters per second squared); per point: `var p_node int[>..]` (global glTF node), `var p_j int[>..]` (4 per point), `var p_w f32[>..]` (4 per point), `var p_md f32[>..]`, `var p_damp f32[>..]`, `var p_grav f32[>..]`, `var p_rest float3[>..]`, `var p_x float3[>..]`, `var p_prev float3[>..]`, `var p_anc float3[>..]`, `var p_q linalg::Quat[>..]`, `var p_im f32[>..]` (0 for pinned points, else 1), `var p_g int[>..]` (the group each point belongs to, counting from 0), `var p_y f32[>..]` (the point's height within its group at rest, 0 at the lowest point and 1 at the highest), `var p_tie int[>..]` (its tether point, -1 for none), `var p_tl f32[>..]` (tether length), `var p_cap int[>..]` (2 per point: its nearest capsules, -1 for none), `var p_hv int[>..]` (2 per point: indices into the contact list, -1 for none); contacts `var under int[>..]` (the nodes of the garments worn under simulated fabric: every wear whose class is neither simulated nor "rigid"); `var h_v int[>..]` (global vertex of the body's "skin" mesh or of a garment in `under`), `var h_p float3[>..]`, `var h_n float3[>..]` (their skinned position and normal this frame), `var h_f f32[>..]` (1, or -1 for a contact whose vertex normal faces away from the fabric: trims and lace are modeled with both faces); `var ngar = 0` (groups built), `var cl_asset = -1` (set to the asset at the start of `cloth_setup`); neighbours for shape matching as `var nb_first int[>..]` (npts + 1 offsets) and `var nbr int[>..]`; edges `var e_a int[>..]`, `var e_b int[>..]`, `var e_l f32[>..]`, `var e_k f32[>..]`; caps `var c_a int[>..]`, `var c_b int[>..]`, `var c_r0 f32[>..]`, `var c_r1 f32[>..]`; `var pal gfx::mat4[>..]`.

While `setup_group` builds a group, these module `var`s hold it, and `setup_group` clears every one of them first: `var g_v int[>..]` (the group's global vertices in collection order), `var g_c int[>..]` (each vertex's fabric row), `var g_lab int[>..]` (each vertex's cage point), `var g_pos float3[>..]` (each cage point's rest position), `var g_cls int[>..]` (each cage point's fabric row), `var g_ea int[>..]`, `var g_eb int[>..]`, `var g_el f32[>..]` and `var g_ek f32[>..]` (the group's edges as cage point pairs with rest length and stiffness: the structural edges first, then the bend edges), `var g_ns = 0` (the number of structural edges), `var g_aj int[>..]` and `var g_aw f32[>..]` (4 anchor joints and weights per cage point), `var g_md f32[>..]` (each cage point's max swing), `var g_h0 int[>..]` and `var g_h1 int[>..]` (each cage point's two contacts, -1 for none), `var g_tie int[>..]` and `var g_tl f32[>..]` (each cage point's nearest pinned cage point along the cloth and that path length).

`hv_add(v int, c float3, two const int[:]) int` returns the index of vertex v in `h_v`, looking it up with a plain loop first; when it is missing it pushes v into `h_v`, zero vectors into `h_p` and `h_n`, and into `h_f` -1 when `dot3(vnorm(v), c - vpos(v)) < 0` and v is one of two, else 1, and returns the new last index.

`cage_points(sim int[:], sim_class int[:]) int` fills `g_v`, `g_c`, `g_lab`, `g_pos` and `g_cls` and returns the number of cage points m. The group's vertices: for each k in order, the mesh of node `sim[k]` (skip a node without one), each of its primitives in order, each vertex v of the primitive in order: push global vertex `prim.vfirst + v` into `g_v` and `sim_class[k]` into `g_c`. Return 0 when there are none. Seeds: in vertex order, accept a vertex's position as a new seed when no seed accepted so far is strictly closer to it than the cell (`f_cell`) of that vertex's class (a plain loop over the seeds). m is the seed count. Label every vertex with its nearest seed (the first one on ties) into `g_lab`. Each cage point's rest position is the sum of its labelled vertices' positions divided by their count as f32 (a count of at least 1), and its class is the fabric row with the most votes among its labelled vertices (the lowest row on ties).

`cage_edges(sim int[:], m int)` builds the group's edges into `g_ea`, `g_eb`, `g_el` and `g_ek` and sets `g_ns`. Adjacency is an m * m `bool` list: walk the same nodes, primitives and triangles as `cage_points` with a running offset `base` that starts at 0 and grows by each primitive's vertex count once that primitive is done; for each triangle t of a primitive (corner q at `gltf::indices[prim.ifirst + 3 * t + q]` as int, local to the primitive) take the labels `g_lab[base + corner]` of its three corners, and for each of its three edges (corner e and corner (e + 1) % 3) whose labels a and b differ, set both `adj[a * m + b]` and `adj[b * m + a]`. Structural edges: for i from 0 below m and j from i + 1 below m with adjacency, push i, j, the distance between their rest positions and stiffness 1.0; `g_ns` is their count. Bend edges follow them: with a second m * m `bool` list `done`, for i from 0 below m, for each j from 0 below m adjacent to i, for each k from 0 below m with k greater than i, k adjacent to j, k not adjacent to i and `done[i * m + k]` not set: set it and push i, k, the distance between their rest positions and stiffness `(f_bend[class of i] + f_bend[class of k]) * 0.5`.

`cage_weights(asset int, m int)` fills `g_aj` and `g_aw`. With `nj` the joint count of skin `sk`, a list `wacc` of m * nj zeros: for each group vertex i (its floats at `g_v[i] * gltf::VERTEX_FLOATS`) and q from 0 to 3, the joint `ji` is float `18 + q` as int and the weight is float `22 + q`; when ji is at least 0 and below nj, add the weight to `wacc[g_lab[i] * nj + ji]`. Skin joint k is a leg joint when its node (`gltf::ints[joints.first + k]`) or one of that node's ancestors (follow `gltf::nodes[node].parent` while it is not negative) is the node `tl = gltf::find_node(asset, "thigh_l")` or `tr = gltf::find_node(asset, "thigh_r")`. With `pjt = joint_index(gltf::find_node(asset, "pelvis"))`, for each cage point i: copy its row of wacc into a list `tw` while summing the weights of its non-leg joints into `kept`; when kept is above 0, set the leg joints' entries of tw to 0 (fabric hangs from the hips and the legs move it only by contact). Pick four: four times, scan j from 0 below nj for the largest entry (a best weight starting at -1.0, replaced only by a strictly larger one); when one is found record its index and weight, add the weight to `wsum` and set that entry to -1.0, else record index 0 and weight 0.0. When wsum is above 0 push the four indices into `g_aj` and each weight divided by wsum into `g_aw`; else push `max(pjt, 0)` with weight 1.0 and then index 0 with weight 0.0 three times.

`cage_transfer(asset int, m int)` gives loose fabric the skinning of the body it rests on (skin weight transfer with inpainting, the way production pipelines copy a body's weights onto its clothes): it replaces the anchor joints and weights in `g_aj` and `g_aw` of every cage point whose class name is "loose" (`fabrics[g_cls[i]].name == "loose"`), and returns at once when no cage point is loose. Body vertices `bv`: `mesh_verts` of the mesh of the node `gltf::find_node(asset, "skin")` when it is found, then `mesh_verts` of the mesh of each node in `under`, in order. With `nj` the joint count of skin `sk`, a list `W` of m * nj zeros and a `bool` list `fixed` of m falses: for each loose cage point i, keep the eight body vertices nearest its rest position `g_pos[i]` (distances in `bd f32[8]` starting at 1.0e30 and vertices in `bi int[8]` starting at -1: for each vertex g of bv in order with distance d, when d is below `bd[7]` put it in slot 7, then while the slot q is above 0 and `bd[q]` is below `bd[q - 1]` swap the two slots and step q down); when `bi[0]` is at least 0 and `bd[0]` is below 0.015 the point is fixed: for each slot q whose vertex is at least 0, add `w = 1.0 / max(bd[q] * bd[q], 0.00000001)` to `total` and, for c from 0 to 3 with j the vertex's float `18 + c` as int, when j is at least 0 and below nj add `w` times the vertex's float `22 + c` to `W[i * nj + j]`; then divide the point's nj entries by `max(total, 0.00000001)`. Inpainting (points the body does not touch take the mean of their neighbours, so the weights spread smoothly from the contact region): 300 times, with a second list `W2` of m * nj zeros made once before the passes, first for each loose cage point i that is not fixed and has at least one structural neighbour (for each structural edge e in order, the first `g_ns` group edges, its other end when one end is i; a neighbour counts once per edge), set each `W2[i * nj + j]` to the sum of `W[k * nj + j]` over those neighbours k in edge order divided by their count as f32; then copy the nj entries of W2 into W for those same points. Finally for each loose cage point i whose nj entries of W sum to more than 0.000001: when it is free (`g_md[i]` at least 0.0001) and both `jl = joint_index(gltf::find_node(asset, "thigh_l"))` and `jr = joint_index(gltf::find_node(asset, "thigh_r"))` are at least 0, the front of a skirt below the hip joints follows the two thighs (sitting lays it on the lap): with `hip = pos(linalg::inverse(ibm_of(gltf::find_node(asset, "thigh_l"))))`, `pz` the z of `pos(linalg::inverse(ibm_of(pv)))` with `pv = gltf::find_node(asset, "pelvis")` (0.0 when pv is negative), `fr = clamp01((g_pos[i].z - pz) / 0.02)`, `hf = clamp01((hip.y - g_pos[i].y) / 0.02)` and `k = clamp01(fr * hf)`, when k is above 0: with `ts` the sum of the point's nj entries of W in order, set each entry to `W[i * nj + j] * (1.0 - k) / max(ts, 0.000001)`, then add `0.5 * k` to entry jl and to entry jr. Then pick its four largest entries (four times, scan j from 0 below nj for the largest entry, a best weight starting at -1.0 and replaced only by a strictly larger one; when one is found record its index and weight, add the weight to `wsum` and set that entry to -1.0, else record index 0 and weight 0.0) and write the four indices into `g_aj[4 * i + q]` and each weight divided by wsum into `g_aw[4 * i + q]`.

`cage_reach(asset int, sim_class int[:], still int[:], m int)` fills `g_md`, `g_h0` and `g_h1`. Body vertices `sv`: `mesh_verts` of the mesh of the node `gltf::find_node(asset, "skin")` when it is found; still vertices `stv`: `mesh_verts` of the mesh of each node in still. `lo` is true when the class name of any row in sim_class is "loose". Under vertices `uv`: for each node in `under` that is not in still, when lo is true, its mesh's `mesh_verts`; then push every vertex of uv onto sv. `ptop` and `plow` are the largest and smallest y of the cage points' rest positions. For each cage point i with rest position `c` and class `cl`: the gap is the smallest distance from c to the position of any vertex of sv and then of stv, and `ns` is the vertex of sv nearest to c (the first one on ties, -1 when sv is empty). Its max swing is `(if the class name of cl is "loose" { clamp01((ptop - 0.25 * (ptop - plow) - c.y) / (0.35 * (ptop - plow) + 0.000001)) } else { 1.0 }) * min(f_swing[cl], f_gain[cl] * max(gap - 0.01, 0.0))` (a skirt's fitted yoke: its top quarter stays pinned to the body and the swing eases in over the next 35 percent of its height), pushed into g_md. Contacts h0 and h1 start at -1; when ns is at least 0: `nrm` is `vnorm(ns)`, replaced by `unit3(c - vpos(ns))` when the gap is at least 0.00001; the high vertex is the vertex v of sv strictly within 0.07 of c with the largest `dot3(vpos(v) - c, nrm)` (scan sv in order from a best of -1.0e30, replaced only by a strictly larger value; -1 when none); `h0 = hv_add(ns, c, uv)`, and when high is at least 0 and differs from ns, `h1 = hv_add(high, c, uv)`. Push h0 into g_h0 and h1 into g_h1.

`cage_tethers(m int)` fills `g_tie` and `g_tl` with a shortest-path search along the structural edges (the first `g_ns` group edges) from all pinned cage points at once (Dijkstra with a plain scan): a cage point is pinned when its max swing is below 0.0001; pinned points start with length 0.0 and themselves as tie point, the others with 1.0e30 and -1, and none is settled. Repeat: pick the unsettled point with the smallest length below 1.0e29 (scan i from 0, replaced only by a strictly smaller length; stop when there is none) and settle it; then for each structural edge e in order, the other end is the second end when the first end is the picked point, else the first end when the second end is the picked point, else there is none; when there is an other end, it is unsettled and the picked length plus `g_el[e]` is below its length, set its length to that sum and its tie point to the picked point's tie point.

`cage_joints(asset int, m int)` turns the cage points into new joints of skin `sk` and re-skins the group's vertices to them. With `pv = gltf::find_node(asset, "pelvis")`, `ib = ibm_of(pv)`, `nj` the skin's joint count before any change and `node_base = gltf::nodes.len`: for each cage point k push `gltf::Node { parent: pv, mesh: -1, skin: -1, children: gltf::Span { 0, 0 }, t: linalg::point(ib, g_pos[k]), r: mquat(ib), s: float3 { 1.0, 1.0, 1.0 }, matrix: gfx::mat4_identity, has_matrix: false, weights: gltf::Span { 0, 0 }, name: gltf::Span { 0, 0 }, .. }`. Then rebuild three spans at the end of their arrays: the pelvis's children (push its old children onto `gltf::ints`, then `node_base + k` for every k, and set the span to the new first index with the old count plus m), the skin's joints the same way, and its inverse binds (push the old matrices onto `gltf::matrices`, then `gfx::translate(g_pos[k] * -1.0)` for every k); add m to `gltf::assets[asset].nodes.count`. Re-skin each group vertex g: keep its four nearest cage points in `bd f32[4]` (starting at 1.0e30) and `bi int[4]` (starting at -1): for each cage point c in order with distance d from the vertex position, when d is below `bd[3]` put it in slot 3, then while the slot q is above 0 and `bd[q]` is below `bd[q - 1]` swap the two slots and step q down; then the weight of each slot whose index is at least 0 is `1.0 / (dd * dd)` with `dd = max(bd[q], 0.0001)`, summed into `total`; write `(nj + max(bi[q], 0)) as f32` into the vertex's float `18 + q` and `ws[q] / total` (0.0 when total is 0) into float `22 + q`.

`setup_group(asset int, sim int[:], sim_class int[:], still int[:])` builds one cage shared by the simulated garment nodes in sim (`sim_class[k]` the fabric row of `sim[k]`; `still` lists the group's unsimulated nodes). With `base0 = p_x.len`: clear the group-build `var`s and set `g_ns = 0`; `m = cage_points(sim, sim_class)`, returning when it is 0; then `cage_edges(sim, m)`, `cage_weights(asset, m)`, `cage_reach(asset, sim_class, still, m)`, `cage_transfer(asset, m)` and `cage_tethers(m)`; `node_base = gltf::nodes.len`; `cage_joints(asset, m)`. `top` and `bot` are the largest and smallest y of the group vertices' positions. Append each cage point k in order: `ngar` into `p_g`, `(g_pos[k].y - bot) / max(top - bot, 0.000001)` into `p_y`, `node_base + k` into `p_node`, its four anchor joints and weights into `p_j` and `p_w`, its max swing into `p_md`, `f_damp` and `f_grav` of its class into `p_damp` and `p_grav`, its rest position into `p_rest`, `p_x`, `p_prev` and `p_anc`, `mquat(gfx::mat4_identity)` into `p_q`, into `p_im` 0.0 when its max swing is below 0.0001 and else 1.0, into `p_tie` and `p_tl` -1 and 0.0 when its max swing is below 0.0001 or its tie point is -1 and else `g_tie[k] + base0` and `g_tl[k]`, -1 twice into `p_cap`, and `g_h0[k]` and `g_h1[k]` into `p_hv`. Then append each group edge: `g_ea[e] + base0`, `g_eb[e] + base0`, `g_el[e]` and `g_ek[e]` into `e_a`, `e_b`, `e_l` and `e_k`. Add 1 to `ngar`.

`ibm_of(node int) gfx::mat4` is the node's inverse bind matrix in skin `sk` (`gltf::matrices[inverse_binds.first + k]` for the joint k with `gltf::ints[joints.first + k] == node`), or the identity when it is not a joint.

`cap_r(a int, b int, k f32) float2` measures the body around the segment from joint a to joint b in bind space (positions `pos(linalg::inverse(ibm_of(x)))`): over the vertices of the asset's "skin" node mesh whose weights on the skin joints of a and b add up to at least 0.6, with `t` the projection onto the segment (skip outside [0, 1]) and `r` the distance from it, collect r for t < 0.35 into `lo` and t > 0.65 into `hi`; return `(min(q(lo), q(hi) * 1.1), q(hi))` with `q` the k quantile (sort ascending with insertion sort, element at `floor(len * k)` clamped; 0.07 when empty).

`cloth_setup(path const byte[:], asset int) int` runs before the avatar is added to the scene. Once `sk` is known and before any group is built, it rebuilds `under`: cleared, then the node of every wear whose node is found, whose class row is found, whose class is not simulated and whose class name is not "rigid". Set `cl_asset = asset`. Build the fabric and wear tables, then read the per-model file with the unqualified `read_file(path, data)` into a `byte[>..]` and apply its lines when that works. Set `sk` like the spring module does (the skin of the "skin" node when it has a mesh and skin, else of the first node with both; return 0 when none). For each distinct group name in wear-row order: its rows whose mesh some asset node carries (the node whose mesh name equals the row's mesh); the simulated ones (class with sim true) go in a list with their class rows, the others in `still`; call `setup_group` when the simulated list is not empty. When any points were made, add the caps for the joint pairs (thigh_l, calf_l), (thigh_r, calf_r), (calf_l, foot_l), (calf_r, foot_r), (upperarm_l, lowerarm_l), (upperarm_r, lowerarm_r), (lowerarm_l, hand_l), (lowerarm_r, hand_r) whose nodes exist, with radii `cap_r(a, b, 0.99)`, and one for (thigh_l, thigh_r) with both radii the second value of `cap_r(pelvis, pelvis, 0.95)` measured along the thigh_l to thigh_r segment (use that segment for t and r, and the pelvis joint's weight for the 0.6 test). Link each point to its two nearest caps by rest distance (bind-space joint positions `pos(linalg::inverse(ibm_of(x)))`, distance from the segment minus the radius at the closest point of the segment) into `p_cap`, skipping caps the point already lies inside at rest (that distance below 0), since such a cap does not describe the body under it. Build the neighbour lists from the edges with stiffness at least 0.99 (both directions; count per point, prefix sums into nb_first, then fill). Set `npts` and return it.

`cloth_open(it int)` sets `item = it`.

`anchors()` sets `motion::joints(sk, pal)`, calls `contacts()` and then, for every point i, `p_anc[i]` to the sum over its four joints of `p_w[4 i + c] * linalg::point(pal[p_j[4 i + c]], p_rest[i])`.

`contacts()` skins the contact vertices for this frame: for each k, with v = `h_v[k]` and its floats at `v * gltf::VERTEX_FLOATS`, `h_p[k]` is the sum over its four joints of `weight * linalg::point(pal[joint], position)` and `h_n[k]` the unit sum of `weight * linalg::qrotate(mquat(pal[joint]), normal)` times `h_f[k]`.

`collide(i int, s f32)` pushes point i out of the body near it. For each of its two capsules z that exists: with `A = pos(motion::world[c_a[z]])`, `B = pos(motion::world[c_b[z]])`, `ab = B - A`, `t = clamp01(dot3(x - A, ab) / max(dot3(ab, ab), 0.000001))`, `c = A + ab * t`, `r = (c_r0[z] + (c_r1[z] - c_r0[z]) * t + 0.006) * s`, `d = x - c`, `l = length3(d)`: when `l < r`, set `x = c + (if l > 0.000001 { d / l } else { unit3(p_anc[i] - c) }) * r`. For each of its two contacts h that exists: with `e = dot3(x - h_p[h], h_n[h])` and the sideways offset `u = length3(x - h_p[h] - h_n[h] * e)`, the contact applies only to fabric right over it (`u < 0.025 * s` and `e > -0.05 * s`); then when `e < 0.004 * s` move x by `h_n[h] * (0.004 * s - e)`. Store x back into `p_x[i]`.

`tether(i int, s f32)` keeps point i within its tether length of its tether point: when `p_tie[i] >= 0`, with `e = p_x[i] - p_x[p_tie[i]]`, `l = length3(e)` and `m = p_tl[i] * s * 1.02`, when `l > m` set `p_x[i] = p_x[p_tie[i]] + e * (m / l)`.

`limit(i int, s f32)` keeps point i within its max distance of its anchor: `e = p_x[i] - p_anc[i]`, `l = length3(e)`, `m = p_md[i] * s`; when `l > m`, `p_x[i] = p_anc[i] + (if l > 0.000001 { e * (m / l) } else { e })`.

`rotations(its int)` finds each point's rotation by shape matching with its neighbours (the method of Müller et al. 2016): for each point i, build the three columns of `A = sum over neighbours j of (p_x[j] - p_x[i]) * transpose(p_rest[j] - p_rest[i])`, that is column c is `sum (p_x[j] - p_x[i]) * (p_rest[j] - p_rest[i]).c` for c in x, y, z; then `its` times: with `q = p_q[i]`, `r0 = qrotate(q, v3(1, 0, 0))`, `r1 = qrotate(q, v3(0, 1, 0))`, `r2 = qrotate(q, v3(0, 0, 1))`, `om = (cross3(r0, a0) + cross3(r1, a1) + cross3(r2, a2)) * (1 / (abs(dot3(r0, a0) + dot3(r1, a1) + dot3(r2, a2)) + 0.000000001))`, `w = length3(om)`; stop when w < 0.000000001; else `p_q[i] = linalg::qnorm(linalg::qmul(linalg::qaxis(om / w, w), q))`.

`cloth_step(dt f32)` returns when `npts == 0`, or when the placement scale `s = length3(s3d::items[item].place.c0.xyz)` is below 0.001 (no pose from the page yet). Call `anchors()`. When `!ready || dt <= 0`: set every `p_x` and `p_prev` to its anchor; when `!ready`, set every `p_q` to `mquat(motion::world[p_node[i]])` composed after nothing (just that), run `rotations(20)`, and set `ready = true`. Otherwise, with `h = min(dt, 1 / 30)` and 3 substeps of `hs = h / 3`: each substep, for every point: when `p_im[i] == 0`, `p_prev[i] = p_x[i]` and `p_x[i] = p_anc[i]`; else `v = (p_x[i] - p_prev[i]) * p_damp[i]`, `p_prev[i] = p_x[i]`, `p_x[i] = p_x[i] + v + (v3(0, -9.81, 0) * p_grav[i] + wind) * (s * hs * hs)`. Then 7 iterations of: every edge with `wa = p_im[e_a]`, `wb = p_im[e_b]`, `sw = wa + wb`, skipping when sw is 0: `d = p_x[b] - p_x[a]`, `l = length3(d)`, skipping when l < 0.000000001; `c = d * ((l - e_l[k] * s) / l * e_k[k] / sw)`; `p_x[a] = p_x[a] + c * wa`; `p_x[b] = p_x[b] - c * wb`; then every point with `p_im[i] > 0`: `tether(i, s)`, `limit(i, s)` and `collide(i, s)` (the body wins over the swing limit). After the substeps run `rotations(3)`.
Finally pose the joints: for each point i with node n and parent `pn = gltf::nodes[n].parent` (skip when pn < 0): `wp = motion::world[pn]`, `iw = linalg::inverse(wp)`, `ps = length3(wp.c0.xyz)`; call `s3d::set_node(item, n, linalg::point(iw, p_x[i]), linalg::qnorm(linalg::qmul(qconj(mquat(wp)), p_q[i])), v3(s / ps, s / ps, s / ps))`.

`cloth_stats(out f32[:])` reports how each garment behaves in the current pose, for graders: `out[0] = ngar`; for each group g, at `1 + 4 g`: the number of its points, its flare and the number and deepest of its points inside the body. Flare: with `c = pos(motion::world[pelvis])` (the node `gltf::find_node(cl_asset, "pelvis")`, a global index; report flare 1 when it is missing), over the garment's points with `p_y < 0.15`, the mean horizontal distance (x and z) of `p_x` from c divided by the mean horizontal distance of `p_anc` from c (1 when there are none or the second mean is 0): above 1 the hem stands out further than the skinned body would carry it. Inside: a point is inside when it is inside one of its two linked caps (its distance from the cap's segment, as in `collide` with the placement scale s but without the 0.006 margin, is below the radius) or behind one of its contacts where that contact applies (as in `collide`: sideways offset below 0.025 s, `e` above -0.05 s, and `e < 0`); count those points and keep the deepest such depth (in meters, divided by s).

`cloth_wind(x f32, y f32, z f32)` sets `wind`.


